xtensa: coprocessors enabled from reset, as in Espressif's QEMU

Upstream QEMU sets CPENABLE to 0xff at CPU reset only in user mode; in
system mode it stays 0, so the FPU is off until software turns it on.
ESP-IDF turns it on lazily, from the coprocessor exception, for a running
task. Floating point before the scheduler starts, in a global constructor,
therefore took an exception ESP-IDF treats as fatal ("Coprocessor
exception"): the library's stepper-28byj sketch crashed at start-up on the
S3 (AccelStepper's constructor). Espressif's QEMU starts both the ESP32
and the S3 with CPENABLE at 0xff (read with GDB at the reset vector), and
runs the sketch. Now the fork does too.

Written for esp32bench (GPL-2.0-or-later). Changes QEMU's
target/xtensa/cpu.c (GPL-2.0-or-later, as QEMU). Applied by esp32bench
(core/crates/sim/src/lcgamboa.rs) to lcgamboa/qemu at
885a6e7061fb70fac78a074a719423810bc19a17, after patch 0009.

diff --git a/target/xtensa/cpu.c b/target/xtensa/cpu.c
index b8b2915..9e54e65 100644
--- a/target/xtensa/cpu.c
+++ b/target/xtensa/cpu.c
@@ -118,8 +118,12 @@ static void xtensa_cpu_reset_hold(Object *obj, ResetType type)
         !xtensa_abi_call0()) {
         env->sregs[PS] |= PS_WOE;
     }
-    env->sregs[CPENABLE] = 0xff;
 #endif
+    /* esp32bench: coprocessors (the FPU) enabled from reset in system mode too, as
+     * Espressif's QEMU does. ESP-IDF enables the FPU lazily for a running task, so
+     * floating point in a global constructor (before the scheduler) took a
+     * coprocessor exception it treats as fatal: AccelStepper's constructor on the S3. */
+    env->sregs[CPENABLE] = 0xff;
     env->sregs[VECBASE] = env->config->vecbase;
     env->sregs[IBREAKENABLE] = 0;
     env->sregs[MEMCTL] = MEMCTL_IL0EN & env->config->memctl_mask;
